---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import ExampleTable from '../components/ExampleTable.astro';
import LadderChart from '../components/LadderChart.astro';
import { terra, flash, opus, pool, fallback, catalogVersion, indexVersion, cuts } from '../lib/example-pool';

const models = `${terra.name} (${terra.via}), ${flash.name} (${flash.via}) and ${opus.name} (${opus.via})`;

const config = `"router": {
  "decider": { "kind": "llm", "profile": "${flash.id}" },
  "pool": [${pool.map((p) => `"${p.id}"`).join(', ')}],
  "default": "${fallback.id}"
}`;

const app = `from openai import OpenAI

client = OpenAI(base_url="http://127.0.0.1:4141/v1", api_key="unused")
reply = client.chat.completions.create(
    model="auto",
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Rename foo to bar in utils.py"}],
)`;

const link =
  'underline underline-offset-4 decoration-[var(--color-line)] text-[var(--color-ink)] transition-colors duration-300 ease-base hover:decoration-[var(--color-ink)]';
const btn = 'btn inline-block px-3 py-2 text-base font-semibold';
const stepNum = 'font-mono text-[var(--color-ink-soft)] text-sm';
const stepTitle = 'text-xl mt-2 mb-2';
const pickRow = 'border-t border-[var(--color-line)] pt-4 grid gap-2 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,20rem)_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-10';
const useRow = 'border-t border-[var(--color-line)] pt-6 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,20rem)_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-10';
---

<BaseLayout path="/">
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="pt-10 pb-16 md:pt-16 md:pb-20">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-6" data-reveal>A model router on your own machine</p>
    <h1
      class="hero-title max-w-2xl text-4xl sm:text-5xl md:text-6xl tracking-tight"
      data-reveal
      style="--d: 0.08s"
    >
      Easy requests go to your cheap models. Hard ones go to your strong ones.
    </h1>
    <p
      class="mt-8 max-w-2xl text-lg md:text-xl text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]"
      data-reveal
      style="--d: 0.16s"
    >
      Your app sends its requests to delegation-kit instead of to one provider. A decider you pick rates
      how hard each request is, and the cheapest of your models that can handle it answers. Every answer
      says which model wrote it, and why.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8" data-reveal style="--d: 0.24s">
      <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-3">
        <a href="/docs/" class={btn}>Get started →</a>
        <a href="#use" class={link}>How you use it ↓</a>
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
        Free and open source under MIT. Runs on your Mac, with your own keys.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-12">
      <ExampleTable
        count={3}
        rotate
        story
        caption={`The answer column cycles through four example pools with explicitly measured efforts; in each, the cheapest model that reaches the request's minimum answers. The setup below uses the first: ${models}.`}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How you use it -->
  <section id="use" class="rule pt-16 pb-20 scroll-mt-20">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl mb-3" data-reveal>How you use it</h2>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-soft)] max-w-xl mb-10" data-reveal style="--d: 0.08s">
      Three steps. In your app, only the address and the model name change.
    </p>
    <ol role="list" class="grid gap-10">
      <li class={useRow} data-reveal>
        <div>
          <span class={stepNum} aria-hidden="true">1</span>
          <h3 class={stepTitle}>Install it</h3>
          <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
            On macOS, with Node.js, jq, git and python3. It sets up the models it knows (Claude and GPT
            through Claude Code and Codex, Meta Muse through Muse Code or its API, DeepSeek, Qwen, GLM, Kimi, Grok,
            MiniMax). Keys go in
            <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em]">~/.config/delegation-kit/.env</code>, a file only you can
            read. <a href="/docs/configuration/#adding-a-model" class={link}>Any other model</a> with an
            API compatible with OpenAI's can be added, a local one too.
          </p>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock
          label="Terminal"
          typing
          code={`npx delegation-kit
delegation-config show  # your models`}
        />
      </li>
      <li class={useRow} data-reveal>
        <div>
          <span class={stepNum} aria-hidden="true">2</span>
          <h3 class={stepTitle}>Choose your models and the decider</h3>
          <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
            Name the models the router may pick from, the decider that rates each request, and the model
            that answers when the decider can't. Here: {terra.name}, {flash.name} and {opus.name}.
          </p>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock lang="json" label="~/.config/delegation-kit/config.json, next to “profiles”" code={config} />
      </li>
      <li class={useRow} data-reveal>
        <div>
          <span class={stepNum} aria-hidden="true">3</span>
          <h3 class={stepTitle}>Start it and point your app at it</h3>
          <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
            Anything that uses OpenAI's Chat Completions API works: set the base URL to the router and the
            model to <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em]">"auto"</code>. The answer comes back as usual; its
            <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em]">X-Router-Model</code> header names the model. An agent loop
            that sends <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em]">X-Router-Conversation</code> stays on the model
            its first request got. Claude Code and Codex speak other formats: they can't send their
            requests here, but the router can pick them to answer.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid gap-4 min-w-0">
          <CodeBlock label="Terminal" code="delegation-router serve" />
          <CodeBlock lang="python" label="Your app (Python, OpenAI SDK)" code={app} />
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-10" data-reveal>
      <a href="/docs/" class={btn}>Get started, step by step →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- How it picks a model -->
  <section id="how" class="rule pt-16 pb-20 scroll-mt-20">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl mb-10" data-reveal>How it picks a model</h2>
    <ol role="list" class="grid gap-8">
      <li class={pickRow} data-reveal>
        <div>
          <span class={stepNum} aria-hidden="true">1</span>
          <h3 class={stepTitle}>Measured efforts get a score</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-soft)] max-w-2xl">
          Artificial Analysis publishes a versioned set of model tests. Unmeasured efforts stay unknown. Within 8
          points of the day's best: frontier. Within 20: mid. The rest: small.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class={pickRow} data-reveal>
        <div>
          <span class={stepNum} aria-hidden="true">2</span>
          <h3 class={stepTitle}>Your decider rates the request</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-soft)] max-w-2xl">
          It reads an excerpt, never your keys or your list of models, and answers: how hard is this, 0
          to 4, half points allowed? Code, math, rare knowledge?
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class={pickRow} data-reveal>
        <div>
          <span class={stepNum} aria-hidden="true">3</span>
          <h3 class={stepTitle}>The cheapest one strong enough answers</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-soft)] max-w-2xl">
          The rating sets a minimum score: none for 0–1, {cuts.mid} for 2, {cuts.frontier} for 3–4,
          in between for half points. Math or rare knowledge: {cuts.mid} at least. The cheapest of your
          models above it answers; none above? Your strongest. No rating? Your default.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-4" data-reveal>Intelligence Index v{indexVersion} · {catalogVersion}</p>
      <LadderChart />
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
        Ringed dots use the efforts in this example; other dots use each model's best measured setting.
        Unmeasured results stay off the chart. The amber line is a request's minimum score: the
        cheapest ringed model at or above it answers. Refresh the scores with your own Artificial
        Analysis key, or set any model's tier yourself.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <h3 class="text-2xl mb-6" data-reveal>With your three models</h3>
      <ExampleTable
        why
        caption="Costs are the router's estimates at API prices, used to compare models; a model you reach through a subscription is billed by your plan."
      />
    </div>
    <p class="mt-12 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2">
      <a href="/docs/" class={link}>Get started →</a>
      <a href="/docs/router/" class={link}>Router reference →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/motion';
</script>

<style>
  /* An ink button: paper text on ink. It lifts to the next dark step on
   * hover and gives a little on press. */
  .btn {
    background: var(--color-ink);
    color: var(--color-paper);
    transition:
      background-color 0.3s var(--ease-base),
      transform 0.3s var(--ease-base);
  }
  .btn:hover {
    background: var(--color-ink-hover);
  }
  .btn:active {
    transform: scale(0.98);
  }

  /* The hero line: ink fading to grey, left to right, on the text only. */
  .hero-title {
    background: linear-gradient(to right, #000000, #666666);
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    background-clip: text;
    color: transparent;
  }
</style>
